SELECT: Command 

 SELECT is a SQL command used to retrieve data from one or more tables in a database. It is a read-only operation, meaning it does not modify the data. In my current role, I use SELECT queries to validate customer information, investigate production issues, verify data before making updates, and generate reports.

SELECT only retrieves data. It does not insert, update, or delete records.
Yes. By using JOINs, SELECT can retrieve related data from multiple tables.

I use SELECT almost every day to verify customer records, validate application data, investigate production issues, check user account details, verify onboarding information, and generate reports for stakeholders.

SELECT Name,
       Department,
       Salary
FROM Employee;

SELECT * retrieves all columns from a table, whereas SELECT Name retrieves only the Name column. In production, I prefer selecting only the required columns because it improves query performance and reduces unnecessary data retrieval.


SELECT *
FROM Users
WHERE Email='john@gmail.com';

